[size=5][b]Client & modeling update[/b][/size]

Since the last update, most of the client-side work went into two things: getting the native Rust client to run the Tower's first floor end to end, and a set of model studies for the Tower's bosses, characters and lobby. Every image below is a real model or a real client capture. "Game-style offline render" means the frames were drawn by the Rust client's own renderer and model lighting, outside a live game session.

[b]Native client progress[/b]

The Rust client now runs the Tower's first floor, the Cinderworks, end to end against the server. Working and verified in this period: banking (the bank interface, deposits that survive logout and restart, and the floor's banker with its dialogue), public chat typed through the client and echoed by the server, a working logout button, the character creator for new accounts, and reliable closing of bank interfaces. Start-up to playable improved from about 13.4 s to about 12.2 s in matched captures; the under-10-second target isn't met yet. Most of this is verified by automated headless tests rather than long play sessions, and interface and parity work continues. Both development machines are now building one shared automated in-game test harness, so client checks become routine.

